course: dictionary
lesson: 4230
title: Gran and friendly words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Gran is a friendly, informal word for grandmother. People use it in everyday talk, so it sounds warm rather than formal. Other families say Granny, Grandma or Nan for the same person.
explain: Look at the words around gran to learn more about it. «Gran hands Sam a present, Mum raises an eyebrow and says "What do you say?", and Sam says "Thanks, Gran".» Gran is the person giving the present, and Sam speaks to her directly, so gran is someone the family talks to and cares about.
explain: Gran often appears in everyday phrases such as my gran, Gran's house, Gran and Grandad and visit Gran. Notice that Gran is written with a capital letter when it works as a name, and with a small letter in my gran.
fact: Gran means grandmother.
fact: Gran is a friendly, informal word, so it suits talk with family and friends.
fact: Common phrases are my gran, Gran's house, Gran and Grandad, and visit Gran.
fact: «Thanks, Gran» shows how small polite words make people feel noticed.
fact: Gran's advice «never mix bleach with other cleaners» is completely true, because some mixtures give off poisonous gas.
exercise: «Gran hands Sam a present, and Sam says "Thanks, Gran".» Who is Gran in this sentence? | *Sam's grandmother | Sam's mum | Sam's best friend | Sam's teacher
exercise: What does gran mean? | A large family car | *Grandmother | A school subject | A type of cake
exercise: Which word is a friendly, informal way to say grandmother? | Relative | *Gran | Matriarch | Ancestor
test: In «Gran's "never mix bleach with other cleaners" is completely true», why is the advice true? | Gran has never used bleach | Bleach smells nice | Cleaners always make people sleepy | *Some mixtures give off poisonous gas
test: Why does Mum raise an eyebrow and say "What do you say?" | To tell Sam to leave the room | To ask Gran for a present | *To remind Sam to say thank you | To check the time
exam: Which best describes gran? | A type of bleach | *A friendly, informal word for grandmother | A formal word for a great-aunt | A word only used in one town
